@page "/learn/relative-navigation"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Relative links · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Relative links</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">An order page has tabs. Can each tab link to the others without spelling out <code>/orders/12047/</code>?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Pages &amp; navigation · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>NavLink</code> and <code>NavigateTo</code> get a
        <code>RelativeToCurrentUri</code> option. It makes a link start from the current page instead of the app's
        root. It's off by default. .NET 10 had no such option: you wrote the full path, or built it yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is order 12047, at <code>…/orders/12047/summary</code>. It has three tabs, each a <code>NavLink</code>
        with a short link:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The tabs, written the .NET 10 way</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;nav class="tabs"&gt;
    <span class="err">&lt;NavLink href="summary"&gt;Summary&lt;/NavLink&gt;</span>
    <span class="err">&lt;NavLink href="lines"&gt;Lines&lt;/NavLink&gt;</span>
    <span class="err">&lt;NavLink href="customer"&gt;Customer&lt;/NavLink&gt;</span>
&lt;/nav&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>The demo runs on .NET 11 with the new option off, which behaves as .NET 10 did.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="Tabs written as plain relative links" Url="demo/relative-nav/net10/orders/12047/summary"
                Label='Option off, as in .NET 10' Side="before">
        <Caption>
            No tab is underlined, even though you're on Summary. Click <b>Lines</b>. The address above the frame
            becomes <code>/lines</code>, and you get <b>Not Found</b>.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Links start from the app's root</h2>
    <p>Every Blazor app sets a base URL in its <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. The browser starts relative links from there:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where href="lines" goes</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;base href="/" /&gt;                <span class="dim">in App.razor</span>
current page   /demo/relative-nav/net10/orders/12047/summary
href="lines"   →  /lines           <span class="err">the app's root, not the order</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        So <code>lines</code> means <code>/lines</code>, from any page. That's also why no tab is underlined.
        <code>NavLink</code> underlines a tab when its link is the page you're on. The Summary tab points to
        <code>/summary</code>, which isn't the page you're on. <code>NavigateTo</code> is the same: in .NET 10, <code>NavigateTo("summary")</code> from an
        order goes to <code>/summary</code>, a 404.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Start from the current page</h2>
    <p>Set <code>RelativeToCurrentUri</code> on each link, and pass the same option to <code>NavigateTo</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="lines" <span class="hl">RelativeToCurrentUri="true"</span>&gt;Lines&lt;/NavLink&gt;

<span class="dim">// from …/orders/12047/lines, go to …/orders/12047/summary</span>
Navigation.NavigateTo("summary",
    <span class="hl">new NavigationOptions { RelativeToCurrentUri = true }</span>);</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        With the option on, Blazor drops the last part of the current URL and adds your path. From
        <code>…/12047/summary</code>, <code>lines</code> becomes <code>…/12047/lines</code>. The tabs don't need to
        know the order id.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same tabs, relative to the page" Url="demo/relative-nav/orders/12047"
                Label=".NET 11: RelativeToCurrentUri" Side="after">
        <Caption>
            <b>Summary</b> is underlined. Click <b>Lines</b>. The order's lines show, and the address ends in
            <code>/orders/12047/lines</code>. Click <b>← Orders</b> and open another order. The same tabs work
            for that one too.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Apart from the tabs, the two pages are the same. Here are the tabs:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RelativeLinks/OrderTabs.razor" Feature="relative-navigation">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's tabs: three relative links. They don't need the order id. Fig. 1's tabs are the same without
            <code>RelativeToCurrentUri</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <p>
        One catch. Fig. 2's page was opened at <code>…/orders/12047</code>, with no tab, and redirected itself to
        the Summary tab with <code>NavigateTo</code>. The option drops the last part of the URL, and here that last
        part is the order id:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">NavigateTo with the option on, from three addresses</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">current page          path             goes to</span>
…/orders/12047/lines  "summary"        …/orders/12047/summary  <span class="ok">✓</span>
…/orders/12047/       "summary"        …/orders/12047/summary  <span class="ok">✓</span>
…/orders/12047        "summary"        …/orders/summary        <span class="err">✗ id dropped</span>
…/orders/12047        "12047/summary"  …/orders/12047/summary  <span class="ok">✓ id added back</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>The page can be opened with or without a trailing slash, so it checks for the slash first:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RelativeLinks/OrderPage.razor" Feature="relative-navigation">
        <Caption>
            With a trailing slash it goes to <code>summary</code>. Without one, it goes to
            <code>12047/summary</code>, to put the id back. The tabs don't have this problem. They only show after the
            redirect, so the URL always ends in a tab name.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Write the full path, or build it</h2>
    <p>
        .NET 10 has no option. Here's what three ways of writing a tab did in a .NET 10 build, on
        <code>/orders/12047/summary</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to write a tab in .NET 10, and where it went</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="lines"&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">starts from the app's root, and no tab is underlined</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ /lines: 404 Not found</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="orders/{Id}/lines"&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">works, but every tab needs the id and the full path</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ /orders/12047/lines</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="lines"
  <span class="err">RelativeToCurrentUri="true"</span>&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">compiles, because NavLink passes unknown attributes on to the &lt;a&gt;</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ still /lines, with no warning</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        For <code>NavigateTo</code>, <code>NavigationOptions.RelativeToCurrentUri</code> doesn't compile at all
        (CS0117).
    </p>
    <p>
        So in .NET 10 the only way to link from the current page was to build the URL yourself. You took
        <code>Navigation.Uri</code>, cut it at the last <code>/</code> and added the tab's name, in every component
        that needed it.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One option, on NavLink and NavigateTo</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the option is <code>NavLink.RelativeToCurrentUri</code> on links and
        <code>NavigationOptions.RelativeToCurrentUri</code> for <code>NavigateTo</code>. <code>NavLink</code> builds
        the full URL on the server, so it works on pages with no interactivity. It writes that URL into the link,
        and uses it to pick which tab to underline. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Watch the last part of the URL.</b> It's always dropped, even when it's an id. If a page can be opened with or without a trailing slash, check which before you navigate.</li>
        <li><b>Relative paths only.</b> With the option on, a path starting with <code>/</code>, or a full URL, throws <code>ArgumentException</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Links starting with <code>?</code> or <code>#</code> stay on the page.</b> <code>?tab=2</code> keeps the page and changes only what follows the <code>?</code>. <code>#notes</code> changes only what follows the <code>#</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
